  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 305 AM MST Wed Nov 18 2026/ \n\nA series of Pacific weather systems will continue to move through a \nlarger scale trough in place across the western US through early \nnext week, maintaining unsettled conditions with near to slightly \nbelow average temperatures. A negatively tilted trough continues to \nlift out across central and eastern MT today while surface low \npressure moves to western ND. While the deeper moisture and dynamics \nthat produced heavier snow associated with this system Tuesday \nevening have decreased, moisture continues to wrap around a TROWAL \nfeature across north-central MT this morning where an area of \nwidespread light snow will gradually lift northeastward through \ntoday, with snow ending by mid-day across western portions of \nnorth-central MT but continuing into this afternoon along the Hi-\nline. Gusty northwest winds will be strongest across eastern \nportions of north-central MT, and may combine with falling snow \nthis morning to create additional visibility reductions. \nAdditionally, with temperatures in the 30s gusty winds will \ncreate wind chills in the teens and 20s across much of the area, \nmaking conditions feel more like early winter than late fall, \nespecially after the recent stretch of mild temperatures. Winter \nweather advisories for central MT and eastern portions of SW MT \ncontinue through 11AM with additional light snow accumulation \nmainly early this morning, while advisories for much of the Hi- \nline area continue through this afternoon, where an additional \n1-2 inches is possible before snow ends. \n\nThe next upper level system from the Pacific moves onshore tomorrow \nwith much of its energy continuing east across the Great Basin \nFriday. While the deepest moisture is likely to remain well south \nof the area with this system, some moisture is drawn north to \nproduce some light snow across SW MT, and potentially far north as\ncentral MT later Friday/Friday night. Meanwhile, a colder upper \nlevel low dropping south through western Canada will provide some \nadditional moisture along with a surge of colder air Saturday. A \nshift to cyclonic northwesterly flow as this low descends into the\nregion will increase the chances for upslope generated snow \nacross portions of north-central MT Saturday. At this point \nprecipitation/snow looks to be mainly light but there is still \nconsiderable uncertainty with the timing and extent of moisture \ngoing into this weekend. There is an increasing signal for colder \ntemperatures Saturday and Sunday as cold airmass is generated in \nAlberta and slides south into N-central MT this weekend. Hoenisch",
